            "response_snippet": "To accomplish this task, we can use the `requests` library in Python to send a request to the server and then parse the cookies from the response.\n\nHere's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```python\nimport requests\n\ndef get_session_id(domain):\n    try:\n        headers = {'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/91.0.4472.164 Safari/537.36'}\n        \n        response = requests.get(f'http://{domain}/', headers=headers)\n        cookies = response.cookies\n        \n        session_id = [cookie for cookie in cookies if 'session_id' in str(cookie)][0].value\n        return session_id\n    \n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"An error occurred: {e}\")\n        return None\n\n# usage\ndomain = \"yourdomain.com\"\nprint(get_session_id(domain))\n```\n\nIn this code:\n\n-   We first import the `requests` library, which we will use to send a GET request to the server.\n-   We define a function called `get_session_id` that takes the domain as an argument and returns the session ID.\n-   Inside the function, we create a dictionary of headers where we specify our User-Agent.\n-   We then send a GET request to the specified domain using this header dictionary. The cookies from the response are stored in the `cookies` variable.\n-   We iterate through each cookie and filter those that have 'session_id' in their name. Since the `value` attribute of the Cookie object is a string, we convert it to a string before checking if 'session_id' is in it.\n-   Finally, we return the session ID.\n\nPlease replace `\"yourdomain.com\"` with your actual domain.\n\nAlso, please note that this is just an example code and should be used as a starting point. The specific implementation may vary depending on the requirements of your application.\n\nThis solution does not cover all edge cases and might need adjustments based on how the cookies are stored or sent from the server.",
