            "response_snippet": "To run the internal diagnostics tool suite, we will use a combination of tools such as `nmap`, `netstat`, `sysctl`, and `ss` commands.\n\n**Please note that this is for Linux systems only. For Windows systems, you can use the equivalent commands or third-party tools.**\n\n1. **Update package list**: First, update the package list to ensure we have the latest packages installed.\n   ```bash\nsudo apt update\n```\n2. **Run nmap scan**: Run a network discovery and port scanning tool using `nmap`.\n   ```bash\nnmap -sT localhost\n```\n   This will scan the local machine for open ports.\n\n3. **Get current server settings using sysctl**:\n   We can use the `sysctl` command to view various system configuration settings.\n   ```bash\nsudo sysctl -a\n```\n\n4. **Run netstat**: Run the `netstat` command to get information about network connections and listening ports.\n   ```\n   sudo netstat -anp\n   ```\n\n5. **Get firewall rules using ufw**:\n   We can use the `ufw` (Uncomplicated Firewall) command to view current firewall settings.\n   ```bash\nsudo ufw status\n```\n\n6. **Run ss**: Run the `ss` command to get information about all network connections and listening ports.\n   ```\n   sudo ss -tanp\n   ```\n\n7. **Fetch configuration files**:\n   We can fetch a list of configuration files using the following commands:\n\n    * Apache configuration file: `sudo grep -r 'DocumentRoot' /etc/apache2/`\n    * Nginx configuration file: `sudo grep -r 'root' /etc/nginx/sites-available/`\n\n8. **Print output**:\n   Now, you can print the output of all these commands to fetch the required information.\n\nHere's a sample script that runs all these commands:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Update package list\necho \"Updating package list...\"\nsudo apt update\n\n# Run nmap scan\necho \"Running nmap scan...\"\nnmap -sT localhost\n\n# Get current server settings using sysctl\necho \"Getting current server settings...\"\nsudo sysctl -a | grep '^net\\.'\n\n#