            "response_snippet": "To use the `delete_all_records()` tool, you will need to have a SQL database management system set up on your machine or online platform.\n\nHere are steps to follow:\n\n1. **Check if you have a SQL client:** First, you need a client software that can interact with the database server. There are many options available like DB Browser for SQLite, SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS), MySQL Workbench etc.\n2. **Connect to your database:** Connect to your test database in your chosen client.\n\n3.**Select the table to delete records from and run the tool:**\n\n   Select `delete_all_records()` function in your preferred SQL client and apply it on the table where you want to clear all data. If your database has a user interface, use that as well.\n\n4. **Confirm completion:**\n   Once you've executed `delete_all_records()`, check if all records are deleted by looking at the table in your client or database editor. You can also verify this with a SQL query like `SELECT * FROM table_name` to confirm all rows have been removed.\n\nHere's an example of how it could look like in SQLite using DB Browser for SQLite:\n-   Open your test database with DB Browser.\n-   Switch to the appropriate schema (table) where you want to clear data from.\n-   Look at the `delete_all_records()` option and confirm that you are on a table.\n-   Click \"Apply\" to execute it.\n\nIf you have a more advanced database, refer to your client's documentation for the specific steps.",
